.TH RUNEDOC 1 "2026" "runedoc" "Rune Manual"
.SH NAME
runedoc \- generate the documentation of a Standard ML library
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runedoc
.BI \-\-library " NAME"
.BI \-\-out " DIR"
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B runedoc
.BI \-\-library " NAME"
.BI \-\-examples " DIR"
.br
.B runedoc
.BI \-\-library " NAME"
.BI \-\-laws " DIR"
.br
.B runedoc
.RB ( \-\-page | \-\-dump\-ir | \-\-lint )
.IR file.sml " ..."
.br
.B runedoc
.BI \-\-overview " DIR"
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.BI \-\-library-page " NAME"
.RI [ REL ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runedoc
makes cross-linked Markdown pages from the signatures of a library and the
comments in them: a page for every signature with an entry for each type,
value, exception and substructure it specifies, a page for every functor, an
overview, the structures with the signatures they implement, the exceptions
and who raises them, an index of identifiers and how much is documented. The
Basis Library also has the top-level environment, the types that are one
type, and how it reads its specification. How to read a page is one
.I conventions.md
for the whole tree. It uses the lexer, the parser and the elaborator of
.BR rune (1).
.PP
A library is a directory with a
.B MANIFEST
that lists its files in the order they are compiled in; the names that begin
with
.B Rune
are internal and the rest is documented. Its
.I README.md
is the overview of the library: the heading is its name, and GitHub renders
the file where the sources are browsed. A structure is documented on the page
of the signature it implements, which the comment above it claims with
.B Implements:
and the elaborator checks.
.PP
A comment documents the declaration that follows it, or the constructor or
field it follows on the same line. Plain prose is a valid comment. Code stands
between backquotes, and a name in backquotes that is documented becomes a
link. A description of a function begins with the function applied to
arguments. Paragraphs that begin with a reserved word
.RB ( Raises: ,
.BR Law: ,
.BR Example: ,
.BR Complexity: ,
.BR "See also:" ,
.BR Area: ,
.BR Status: ,
.BR Implements: ,
the notes
.BR Reading: ,
.BR Erratum: ,
.BR Deviation: ,
.B Implementation:
and
.BR Limitation: ,
and
.BR "Pinned by:" )
are checked. The file
.I docs/doc\-comments.md
of the Rune sources describes the language in full.
.PP
The library's file
.B DOCUMENTED
lists the signatures that are documented in full. For them what is only
counted elsewhere is an error: an entry without a comment, a function without
a usage, a reference that leads nowhere.
.PP
Like
.BR rune (1),
the
.B runedoc
on your
.B PATH
is a wrapper script that supplies
.B \-\-lib
for the installed libraries.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-library " NAME"
Document the library in
.IR LIBDIR / NAME .
A
.I NAME
that begins with
.B /
or
.B .
is the directory of the library itself. A library other
than
.IR LIBDIR /basis
is elaborated on top of the Basis Library, so that it may use it, after the
libraries its
.B # library:
lines name. What its comments name of the Basis Library is looked up there.
.TP
.BI \-\-basis\-docs " DIR"
For a library on top of the Basis Library: where the Basis Library's pages
are, from the
.B \-\-out
directory, so that what the library's comments name of it links there.
Without it such a name is shown as code.
.TP
.BI \-\-library\-docs " NAME DIR"
As
.BR \-\-basis\-docs ,
for a library
.I NAME
that this one is written on (its MANIFEST's
.B # library:
lines): where
.IR NAME 's
pages are, from the
.B \-\-out
directory. It may be given for several libraries.
.TP
.BI \-\-out " DIR"
Write the pages to
.I DIR
and remove the pages there that are no longer generated.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Write nothing: fail if
.I DIR
is not what would be written. For a tree of generated pages that is kept under
version control.
.TP
.BI \-\-title " TEXT"
The title of the pages when the library has no
.IR README.md
(default: the name of the library). A
.I README.md
that begins with a heading names the library, and that heading is the title.
When both are given they have to be the same.
.TP
.BI \-\-overview " DIR"
Write
.IB DIR /README.md\fR,
the overview of the libraries named with
.BR \-\-library-page ,
and stop. With
.BR \-\-check ,
fail if that page is not what would be written. The libraries are grouped by
the
.B Area:
of each library's
.IR README.md ,
a row's name is the heading of that file, and a row's text is its first
paragraph. The rest of the file is the long description, which the library's
own page shows after the short one. The area is an HTML comment,
.BR "<!-- Area: NAME -->" ,
so the rendered file is the heading and the prose, or a paragraph
.BR "Area: NAME" .
.TP
.BI \-\-library-page " NAME [REL]"
One library of
.BR \-\-overview .
.I NAME
is read as
.B \-\-library
reads it, and
.I REL
is the directory of its pages under
.IR DIR .
.I REL
defaults to
.IR NAME .
The library's name is the heading of its
.IR README.md .
Repeat the option. The first library of an area places that area, and the
libraries of one area stay in the order they are named.
.TP
.BI \-\-conventions " REL"
Link "how to read these pages" to
.I REL
instead of a
.I conventions.md
in the library's own tree.
.TP
.B \-\-emit-conventions
Also write that page at
.IR REL .
The Basis Library writes the one copy,
.IR docs/lib/conventions.md .
.TP
.BI \-\-tests " DIR"
A test suite whose checks are labelled
.IB Structure . member / case \fR.\fP
Every entry lists the checks of its member,
.B Pinned by:
must name checks that exist, and a value or exception of a signature that has
no check for a structure that implements it is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-annotations " FILE"
Remarks about the members that are made outside the library's sources, as
lines
.IB glob " | " "whom it is about" " | " text \fR,\fP
where the glob is that of the label of a check
.RB ( * ,
.B ?
and
.BR [...] ).
Each is shown under every member that has such a check. A line
.BI @title " TEXT"
names the block they are shown in. A glob that finds no check of a documented
member is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-law\-skips " FILE"
The laws that do not hold in the library's implementation, or are not tested
there, as lines
.IB glob " | " "" "FAILS \fRor\fP UNTESTED" " | " why \fR,\fP
where the glob is that of a law's label at a structure, as
.BR MATH.atan2/law\-1@Real.Math .
Each law is marked on its page, beneath it, with the structures and the
reason. A glob that finds no law is an error. The Basis Library's list is
.BR tests/basis/law\-skips.txt ,
which
.B make test\-laws
reads too.
.TP
.BI \-\-examples " DIR"
Write the examples of the comments, each a claim of type
.BR bool ,
as one program for each signature into
.I DIR
and stop. Each program prints a line
.B PASS
or
.B FAIL
for every example and exits with a failure if one does not hold. A
counterexample, a claim that must not hold, is written into the same program
and passes when its equation's sides have different outcomes or its claim is
false or raises. The same examples and counterexamples are elaborated
whenever the documentation is made.
.TP
.BI \-\-laws " DIR"
Write the laws of the comments as one program for each signature into
.I DIR
and stop. A program holds every law of its signature at every structure that
implements it, each labelled
.IB SIG . member /law- n @ Structure\fR,
with its variables drawn from the arbitraries of the library
.BR test/property ,
and is compiled with
.BR "rune \-\-library test/property/basis" .
It prints a line
.B LAW
before each law and
.B PASS
or
.B FAIL
after it, and exits with a failure if a law does not hold. The same laws are
elaborated whenever the documentation is made; a law that is no Standard ML,
or has a variable no arbitrary draws, is an error at its comment.
.TP
.B \-\-labels
With
.BR \-\-tests :
print the checks of the suite, one on a line, and stop.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-coverage
With
.B \-\-library
and
.BR \-\-tests :
check only that every specified value and exception has a check.
.TP
.B \-\-page
Print the pages of the signatures that the files declare. References between
them resolve, and nothing else does.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ir
Print what is extracted from the files, before any rendering.
.TP
.B \-\-lint
Check that every comment of the files is in the language of doc comments,
also those that document nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-palette " DIR"
Write the palette of the roles that colour Rune's text to
.IB DIR /palette.css
(custom properties and classes, light and dark) and
.IB DIR /palette.json
(the rules of an editor's theme), and stop; with
.BR \-\-check ,
fail if they are not what would be written.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= WHEN
Colour the diagnostics:
.BR always ,
.B never
or
.BR auto ,
the default, which colours them when the standard error is a terminal, as
.BR rune (1)
says.
.TP
.BI \-\-diagnostics= FORM
.B full
shows the lines of source a diagnostic is about under its first line, with
carets under the span;
.B short
shows the first line only,
.IR file : line : col ": error: " message ,
as earlier versions did. The default is full when the standard error is a
terminal and
.B TERM
is not
.BR dumb .
.TP
.BI \-\-diagnostics\-json " FILE"
Also write every diagnostic of the run to
.I FILE
as JSON, in the form rune-ide reads.
.TP
.BI \-\-lib " DIR"
The directory that holds the libraries. The wrapper script passes it; a later
one wins.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B runedoc
reports everything it finds, in the order of the sources, on standard error.
It exits 0 when there was no error (warnings do not count) and 1 otherwise, in
which case it has written nothing.
.SH EXAMPLES
Document a library that uses the Basis Library, and keep the pages checked:
.PP
.nf
.RS
runedoc \-\-library ./mylib \-\-out docs/mylib \-\-title "My library"
runedoc \-\-library ./mylib \-\-out docs/mylib \-\-check
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rune (1),
.BR runevm (1)
